tance-criteria / definition-of-done quality issues.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f607dd457c67f9209","impact":0.4,"is_primary":null,"location":"","norm_id":"ned06ebc801a522e3","priority":0.4,"reason":null,"recalled":false,"scenarios":[],"severity":"minor","suggested_fix":"Split compound criteria, replace subjective terms with observable outcomes, and state how each is verified.","tier":"DET","validity":1.0,"verification":null},{"block_threshold":0.6,"blocking_enabled":true,"checklist_item":"- [ ] State whether nested subparsers are in scope: either propagate RebarHelpFormatter to nested add_parser(...) calls (and have the contract test recurse into subparsers) or explicitly document that only top-level factory formatters are committed/checked and why nested sub-command help is not a py3.13 trap.","cohort":["G6"],"criteria":["G6"],"decision":"dropped","decision_margin":-0.6,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["bridge.py builds nested parsers: commands.add_parser(\"preview\", ...) with no formatter_class, defaulting to argparse.HelpFormatter","Contract test spec: 'iterate the route registry, resolve each parser_factory, and assert its resolved formatter class is a SUBCLASS of RebarHelpFormatter' — operates on the top-level parser only","gen_cli_help.render_route renders parser.format_help() from the top-level factory only, so nested sub-command help is not in committed artifacts today (the trap is narrower than the top level) — but the plan claims 'the trap cannot be reintroduced'"],"finding":"The contract test's trap-prevention guarantee is overstated: bridge's nested subparsers are created via commands.add_parser(...) with no explicit formatter_class (so they fall back to argparse's default HelpFormatter), and the specified test resolves only each route's top-level parser_factory and inspects that parser's formatter class — it never traverses nested subparsers. A nested subparser could reintroduce a non-pinned formatter without failing the test.","group_cr